# RFP Hub /v1/ API — local environment (M2)
#
# THIS IS A REFERENCE LIST. Copy it to `.env` in this directory (packages/api) to have it loaded:
# `src/config.ts` reads that file with dotenv, and `scripts/publish.ts` does the same load on its
# own because it deliberately does not import the server's config. A REAL ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE
# ALWAYS WINS — dotenv never overwrites what already reached the process — so an exported shell
# variable or a deployment's injected value overrides the file rather than the other way round:
#
#   export DATABASE_URL=postgres://rfphub:rfphub@localhost:5432/rfphub
#   pnpm --filter @the-rfp-hub/api migrate
#
# With neither, the code uses its own built-in defaults (localhost) and says so on stderr. To load
# a whole file into the current shell instead of relying on the dotenv load, export every
# assignment in it:
#
#   set -a; . ./.env; set +a
#
# The values below match packages/api/docker-compose.yml. Nothing here is a secret, and nothing
# here names a real deployment: no bucket, no host, no credential.

# Postgres connection string
DATABASE_URL=postgres://rfphub:rfphub@localhost:5432/rfphub

# HTTP server
PORT=3001
HOST=0.0.0.0
# Max size of the pg pool (default 10). Bound it on a shared database instance.
DB_POOL_MAX=10

# Origin the OpenAPI document advertises as servers[0].url. Leave UNSET locally: the default is the
# relative "/", which is correct wherever the server happens to be reachable. A deployed
# environment sets it to the API's OWN origin — never the apex, which is the specification's
# origin — and https is required for any host that is not loopback, because this value tells every
# client which scheme to speak:
#   PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://api.example.org
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# ── Open-data export (pnpm export) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Minimum live entries required to publish. A run below this floor writes NOTHING and exits
# non-zero, so a broken seed cannot replace latest.json/latest.csv with an empty dataset.
EXPORT_MIN_COUNT=100

# ── Publishing the export (pnpm publish:export) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Uploads a FINISHED ./exports directory to an S3 bucket, manifest last. Reads no database.
# Leave every one of these UNSET locally: with no S3_BUCKET the command refuses to run, which is
# the correct behaviour for a laptop — `pnpm export` on its own writes the files and publishes
# nothing.
#
# The bucket NAME only, never a URI or a path — the key prefix is its own variable. NO REAL VALUE
# FOR THIS IS EVER COMMITTED, here or anywhere else in the repo: it names a destination, and a
# destination in version control is a destination somebody publishes to by accident.
#   S3_BUCKET=example-bucket
# S3_BUCKET=

# Optional key prefix; empty means the objects sit at the bucket root. Slashes are normalized, so
# `/open-data/` and `open-data` both put keys under `open-data/`.
# S3_PREFIX=

# Optional. The AWS SDK reads this itself; it is read by the publish step as well so its printed
# plan can name the region the upload will use. Unset falls through to the SDK's own resolution.
# CREDENTIALS are never read by this repo's code at all — they come from the SDK's default chain
# (environment, shared config file, instance role), and nothing about them belongs in this file.
# AWS_REGION=

# `pnpm publish:export --dry-run` prints the exact upload plan — key, content type, cache policy,
# byte size, order — and uploads nothing. It needs no credentials, so it is the safe way to check
# a prefix layout before pointing this at a real bucket.

# That is the whole server config. The seed loader is NOT configured here: it takes a corpus file
# as its argument (`pnpm seed data/seed-corpus.json`) and reads no environment pointer at all
# (`SEED_STRICT=1` only mirrors the `--strict` flag). That
# corpus is a committed repo artifact — RFP Hub Standard documents, curated and reviewed in-tree.
#
# Rebuilding it in bulk from an upstream registry is offline maintainer tooling, run by hand, and
# the only thing here that talks to a network: SOURCE_API_URL (plus optional SOURCE_BRAND /
# CORPUS_SIZE) is read by tools/converter/fetch-corpus.ts straight from the environment. Export it
# in your shell for that one command — it is deliberately not a deployment variable, and no value
# for it is ever committed. See README.md, "Seeding: a static, in-repo corpus".
